          Executive Summary: Workers' Comp Fundamentals
        </div>
        <ul class="callout-list">
          <li><strong>State Rules Kick In Early:</strong> In nearly every U.S. state (Texas is the main exception for private employers), workers' compensation becomes mandatory as soon as you hire your first non-owner employee.</li>
          <li><strong>The Pricing Formula:</strong> Insurers calculate your bill with one core equation: <strong>(Total Gross Payroll &divide; $100) &times; NCCI Class Code Rate &times; Experience Modification Rate (E-Mod)</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Misclassification Carries Heavy Fines:</strong> Listing roofers as office staff to shave down rates is treated as insurance fraud. States respond with stop-work orders, back premiums, and stiff penalty multipliers.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="lead-text">
        Workers' compensation rests on a century-old trade-off called the "grand bargain." Hurt employees get no-fault medical care and partial wage replacement without having to prove anyone was careless, and in return they give up the right to sue their employer in civil court over most workplace injuries.
      </p>

      <h2>Representative NCCI Class Code Rates per $100 of Payroll</h2>
      <p>
        The National Council on Compensation Insurance (NCCI) sorts jobs into 4-digit class codes based on how often workers in that trade get hurt. Here is how typical rates look across common small-business roles:
      </p>

