      <h2>How Your Experience Modification Rating (E-Mod) Multiplies or Cuts Rates</h2>
      <p>
        Once your annual workers' comp premium crosses a set floor (typically $5,000 to $10,000 a year), state rating bureaus assign your company an <strong>Experience Modification Rating (E-Mod)</strong> based on your recent claims history:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Baseline E-Mod (1.00):</strong> Your injury record matches the average for your trade, so you pay the standard rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Favorable E-Mod (e.g., 0.80):</strong> Keep your job sites clean and go three years with zero lost-time claims, and an E-Mod of 0.80 knocks <strong>20% off</strong> your entire workers' comp bill automatically.</li>
        <li><strong>Adverse E-Mod (e.g., 1.45):</strong> Frequent claims push your multiplier up, adding a <strong>45% surcharge</strong> to your premiums. Worse, many commercial general contractors will not even accept bids from subs with an E-Mod above 1.00.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Is a "Ghost Policy" for Sole Proprietors?</h2>
      <p>
        If you work solo as an independent contractor or single-member LLC in construction or landscaping, you have no employees to cover. Even so, general contractors routinely refuse to let you on site without a Workers' Comp Certificate of Insurance.
      </p>
      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">How a Workers' Comp Ghost Policy Works:</h4>
        <p>
          A <strong>Ghost Policy</strong> (or minimum-premium policy) is an active workers' comp policy where you formally exclude yourself as the owner. With zero employees and $0 in covered payroll, you pay only the state minimum premium, usually <strong>$750 to $1,200 a year</strong>.
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          You get a valid Certificate of Insurance so you can bid commercial jobs, and the hiring general contractor stays protected from getting billed for your paychecks during their own year-end audit.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>State-by-State Workers' Comp Employee Thresholds</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1 Employee Required:</strong> California, New York, Illinois, Pennsylvania, Ohio, and New Jersey require coverage as soon as you hire your first worker.</li>
        <li><strong>3 Employees Required:</strong> North Carolina, Georgia, and Florida (non-construction). In Florida construction trades, coverage is mandatory at 1 employee.</li>
        <li><strong>4 to 5 Employees Required:</strong> Mississippi, Alabama, Missouri, and Tennessee.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Worked Payroll Audit Example: How Misclassification Costs Thousands</h2>
      <p>
        Workers' comp overpayments rarely show up on day one. They hit during the year-end payroll audit. Picture a four-person residential remodeling firm with $220,000 in annual payroll: three field carpenters making $170,000 combined and one office bookkeeper earning $50,000:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Unseparated Payroll (Common Mistake):</strong> If clerical wages are not broken out on separate payroll lines, the auditor assigns the entire $220,000 to the carpentry code ($6.80 per $100). Annual base cost: <strong>$14,960</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Properly Segregated Payroll:</strong> Put the $50,000 bookkeeper under NCCI Code 8810 ($0.18 per $100 = $90) and keep $170,000 in carpentry ($11,560). Your bill drops to <strong>$11,650</strong>, saving <strong>$3,310 a year</strong> without switching carriers.</li>
        <li><strong>Overtime Premium Exclusion:</strong> In most NCCI states, only the straight-time portion of overtime pay counts toward workers' comp payroll. If your payroll software dumps overtime bonus pay into regular wages, you quietly overpay at every audit.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Pre-Audit Checklist for Business Owners</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Collect unexpired Certificates of Insurance (COIs) from every 1099 subcontractor before you cut their first check, or your auditor will add their payments straight onto your payroll.</li>
        <li>Keep executive officer pay, office staff, and field labor under separate payroll journal codes all year.</li>
        <li>Double-check your state's officer-exclusion deadline if you are an LLC member or S-Corp owner opting out of personal coverage.</li>
      </ol>

